%S A175808 1,2,3,4,6,6,7,8,11,11,13,13,14,14,15,16,20,20,23,23,25,25,28,28,28,
%T A175808 28,29,29,30,30,31,32,37,37,41,41,44,44,48,48,50,50,52,52,55,55,59,59,
%U A175808 59,59,59,59,59,59,60,60,60,60,61,61,62,62,63,64
%N A175808 a(n) is the length of a shortest common superstring of the binary representations of all natural numbers from 1 to n.
%C A175808 If a(n) = 2^m, then we know that the lexicographically largest superstring coincides with the lexicographically largest de Bruijn sequence, B(2,m) (A166316(m)). - _Thomas Scheuerle_, Oct 09 2021
%C A175808 Conjecture: limsup a(n)/n = 2. - _Liangzhou Chen_, Aug 24 2026
%H A175808 Liangzhou Chen, <a href="/A175808/b175808.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..32768</a>
%F A175808 It appears that a(2^n-1) = 2^n-1 and a(2^n) = 2^n. - _Thomas Scheuerle_, Oct 09 2021
%F A175808 From _Liangzhou Chen_, Aug 24 2026: (Start)
%F A175808 It appears that for N=2^k where k>=2 is an integer, we have:
%F A175808   1. a(n) = N-1-ceiling(log_2(N-1-n)), for n in range [3N/4-1, N-3] (that is, (N-1-n) in range [2, N/4]);
%F A175808   2. a(N-2) = N-2;
%F A175808   3. a(N+1) = N+k.
%F A175808   4. a(2n-1) = a(2n) always holds except when n=2^k. (End)
%e A175808 a(5)=6 because 6 is the length of 110100 or 101100, which are the 2 possible shortest common superstrings of 1,10,11,100,101.
%Y A175808 Cf. A175809 (number of shortest common superstrings).
%Y A175808 Cf. A056744 (least decimal values of shortest common superstrings).
%Y A175808 Cf. A166316, A016031.
